---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Bug1 = `local function double(n)
    print(n * 2)
end

local answer = double(5)

print("Answer plus one: " .. answer + 1)`;

const Bug2 = `local lives = 3

local function loseALife(lives)
    lives = lives - 1
    return lives
end

loseALife(lives)

print("Lives left: " .. lives)`;

const Bug3 = `local function total(t)
    local sum = 0

    for i, v in ipairs(t) do
        sum = sum + v
        return sum
    end
end

print("Total: " .. total({4, 6, 10}))`;

const Bug4 = `print(greet("Ada"))

local function greet(name)
    return "Hello " .. name
end`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp-lua/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Debug Challenge: Functions</h1>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>Objective</h2>

          <p>
            Four programs with functions in them, each with exactly one thing
            wrong. Two crash, two do not, and{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">in three of the four the error message
            names a line that is not where the mistake is.</b>
          </p>

          <p>
            That gap &mdash; between where a program notices and where it went
            wrong &mdash; is most of what makes debugging a skill rather than a
            lookup.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="bugs">
          <h2>The Four Programs</h2>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 1</h3>

          <p>
            This prints <code>10</code> and then stops with an error about
            arithmetic on a <code>nil</code> value.
          </p>

          <Code code={Bug1} frame="code" lang="lua" title="Bug 1" class="mb-3" />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 2</h3>

          <p>
            The player loses a life. It prints{" "}
            <code>Lives left: 3</code>.
          </p>

          <Code code={Bug2} frame="code" lang="lua" title="Bug 2" class="mb-3" />

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            <b>The function is correct.</b> Something around it is not.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 3</h3>

          <p>
            This should add up the table and print <code>Total: 20</code>. It
            prints <code>Total: 4</code>.
          </p>

          <Code code={Bug3} frame="code" lang="lua" title="Bug 3" class="mb-3" />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 4</h3>

          <p>
            This stops immediately with{" "}
            <code>attempt to call global 'greet' (a nil value)</code>.
          </p>

          <Code code={Bug4} frame="code" lang="lua" title="Bug 4" class="mb-3" />
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>For each program, give:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>One sentence on what was wrong</li>
            <li>The corrected line of code</li>
            <li>
              <b>Which line the error names, and which line the mistake is
              on</b> &mdash; for the two that produce errors
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Bug 2 is the one to spend time on.</b>{" "}
            There is nothing wrong inside the function, and writing down exactly
            what happened to the value is worth more than the fix.
          </p>

          <p>Worth <b>5 points</b>, graded on completion.</p>
        </section>

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Commence Debugging
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
